<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody><tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize that people are speaking in English. Interactive: Respond to basic greetings.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize their name when called in English and polite words like "please" and "thank you" (e.g., turning their head, making eye contact, or saying "Yes" when their name is called; smiling or pausing when hearing "thank you").
            Can respond to greetings and polite words in English, such as "hello," "goodbye," "please," and "thank you" (e.g., saying "hello" when greeted, saying "thank you" when given something, or using "please" when prompted).
            Can follow basic instructions paired with gestures, such as "say hello" and "wave" (e.g., waving when asked).
        </td>
    </tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage &amp; Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities &amp; Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the "Hello, My Friends!" song/chant with accompanying TPR actions (waving hello, pointing to a friend, pointing to self). The song introduces the words "hello," "friend," and "happy." The teacher uses a large visual aid (e.g., a chart with simple words and pictures) while singing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of "hello" and "friend" (emphasizing initial /f/) during the song. The teacher points to each word on the chart as it is sung. For "friends," the teacher claps twice to show syllable stress: 'friends' (clap-clap). For "goodbye," the teacher exaggerates the initial /g/ sound and claps twice for 'good-bye'. The teacher models waving and smiling actions for "hello" and "happy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a picture of two children waving) "What do they say? 'Hello' or 'goodbye'?" (Students point or say "Hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a child in the class) "Is this your 'friend'?" (Students nod yes or shake head no).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A "big book" titled "My Happy Friends" with large, colorful illustrations and very short, simple phrases (1-3 words per page). The book features familiar vocabulary: "hello," "friends," "happy," "play," "goodbye," and simple sentences like "I am happy." and "Let's play!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Genre, Register, Format:</b> Picture story, informal/friendly, large-format storybook.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can understand very short, simple texts on familiar topics, supported by pictures (A1.1 Reading). Can recognize their name and other familiar words in English print.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions &amp; Comprehension Tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the "big book" and reads the title, pointing to the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, page by page, pointing to the words while showing the pictures. The teacher uses expressive voice and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> After reading, the teacher asks, "Who is in the story?" (Students point to friends/children in the book). "Are they happy?" (Students nod yes). "What do they do?" (Students might make a 'play' gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word Recognition:</b> The teacher rereads specific pages and asks students to point to specific words: "Show me 'friends'!" (Students point to the word "friends"). "Show me 'happy'!" (Students point to the word "happy"). "Where does it say 'hello'?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill &amp; Content:</b> Reading familiar vocabulary (friend, happy, play, hello, goodbye, please, thank you). Recognizing simple grammar structures (e.g., "I am [name].") within written context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Picture-Word Matching):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures from the "My Happy Friends" story (e.g., a child waving, children playing, a smiling child). Next to each picture are two target vocabulary words (e.g., "hello" and "goodbye" next to the waving child). Students draw a line to match the picture to the correct word. The teacher models the first match.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Word Spotting &amp; Circling):</b> The teacher displays large sentence strips on the board, each containing one simple sentence from the story (e.g., "Hello, friends!," "I am happy," "Let's play!"). The teacher calls out a target word (e.g., "friends"). Students come to the board and circle the word "friends" on the strip. This is repeated for "happy," "play," and "hello."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Sentence Recognition (Yes/No Question with Visual Aid):</b> The teacher shows a flashcard with a simple written sentence like "I am happy." or "I am [name]." (using a generic name). Then, the teacher shows a picture. If the picture matches the sentence (e.g., a happy child for "I am happy."), students give a "thumbs up" and say "Yes!" If it does not match (e.g., a sad child), students give a "thumbs down" and say "No."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Find a Word, Find a Friend" - a guided sight word recognition and interaction activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a large name tag with their name written clearly and a small picture representing a feeling (e.g., happy, nice) or an action (e.g., play, wave). On the back, another target word from the theme (e.g., "hello", "friend") is written.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students walk around the classroom. When they meet a "friend," the teacher instructs them: "Read your friend's name!" or "What does it say?" pointing to the word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to "read" (recognize) their friend's name and then say "Hello, [Name]!" The teacher prompts: "What is your name?" (students point to their tag and say "I am [name].").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then asks students to turn their tags over: "Can you read the word on the back?" (Students identify words like "hello" or "friend"). The teacher can ask: "Who has 'hello'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on recognizing familiar words and names in print and connecting them to basic greetings, reinforcing "What is your name?" and "I am [name]."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Find a Word, Find a Friend" activity and individual check-ins.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, students are presented with three flashcards: one with their name, one with "hello," and one with "friend." The teacher asks, "Show me your name," "Show me 'hello'," and "Show me 'friend'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes and points to their own name in print.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes and points to at least two other target vocabulary words (e.g., "hello," "friend") from the lesson.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows engagement and effort in identifying words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows flashcard "hello") "Did you see 'hello' today? Thumbs up if yes, thumbs down if no."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows flashcard "friend") "Did you see 'friend' today? Thumbs up if yes, thumbs down if no."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you read today that made you happy?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who can you say 'hello' to from the story or our class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific reading activities were most engaging and effective for Pre-K learners? Did they manage to track words from left to right?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the visual aids sufficiently clear and large for word recognition? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with identifying the words? (e.g., more 1-to-1 support, larger fonts, more repetition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did the grammar structures ("I am [name]," "What is your name?") integrate into the reading tasks for this age group?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built foundational word recognition. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking &amp; Interaction) will focus on using these recognized words in oral interactions, practicing greetings and simple self-introductions in pairs, building on the vocabulary and simple sentences learned today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr></tbody></table>